Fix exprtk for nvcc.

unary_vector_node pulls in expression_node<T>::branch via a using-declaration.
That method is declared 'branch(const std::size_t& index = 0)'.  nvcc does not
apply the default argument to a name introduced by a using-declaration, so every
bare branch() call is rejected with 'too few arguments in function call'.  Host
compilers accept it, which is why only the CUDA build hits this.

Spelling the argument explicitly is equivalent for all compilers.  Upstream
already writes branch(0) everywhere else in this class; this is the one call
that was missed.

--- a/exprtk.hpp
+++ b/exprtk.hpp
@@ -14122,7 +14122,7 @@

          inline T value() const exprtk_override
          {
-            branch()->value();
+            branch(0)->value();

             const T* vec0 = vec0_node_ptr_->vds().data();
                   T* vec1 = vds().data();
